INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'dpsa-vacancies-circular-39-2026-20261030', 'global', 'South Africa',
'DPSA Vacancies Circular 39 of 2026: South Africa Government Jobs in 20 Departments',
'',
'South Africa''s DPSA Circular 39 of 2026, published 30 October 2026, gathers government job advertisements from 20 national departments and seven provincial administrations, with highlighted posts closing on 13 November 2026 and 16 November 2026.',
'',
$$<p>South Africa's Department of Public Service and Administration has published Public Service Vacancy Circular 39 of 2026 on 30 October 2026, opening the week's round of <strong>DPSA vacancies</strong> across 20 national departments and seven provincial administrations. The 309-page circular gathers advertisements for administration, finance, human resources, information technology, healthcare, legal and technical roles, from entry-level posts for Matric holders to senior management positions. Closing dates differ by department, and several highlighted posts close on 13 November 2026 or 16 November 2026.</p>
<p>One point should be clear from the start. These are South African public service posts, advertised by the department that will employ the successful candidate, and each advertisement states its own eligibility, qualifications and experience requirements. There is no single application portal for the whole circular: applications go directly to the advertising department, on the prescribed new Z83 application form where the advertisement requires one. The Department of Public Service and Administration issues the circular but does not handle applications or enquiries about individual posts.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Detail</th><th>As published</th></tr>
<tr><td>Publication</td><td>Public Service Vacancy Circular 39 of 2026</td></tr>
<tr><td>Published</td><td>30 October 2026</td></tr>
<tr><td>Length</td><td>309 pages</td></tr>
<tr><td>National departments</td><td>20</td></tr>
<tr><td>Provincial administrations</td><td>7</td></tr>
<tr><td>Application form</td><td>New Z83 form, effective from 1 January 2021, where prescribed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Application route</td><td>Directly to the advertising department</td></tr>
<tr><td>Earliest highlighted closing dates</td><td>13 November 2026 and 16 November 2026, by department</td></tr>
</table>
<h2>DPSA vacancies: the posts drawing attention</h2>
<p>The most accessible post in the circular is the Registry Clerk vacancy at the Department of Communications and Digital Technologies, based in Hatfield, Pretoria. It is listed as Post 39/01, reference REGCLE, and it requires a Grade 12 certificate or National Senior Certificate at NQF level 4, with computer literacy and knowledge of filing and document management. No previous work experience is required. The advertised salary is reported as R237,453 per annum, and the closing date is 16 November 2026. Candidates should confirm the salary figure against the departmental advertisement itself before applying, since the figure as supplied in one republication carried an obvious formatting error.</p>
<p>For graduates and professionals, the circular carries finance and human resources posts with clearly stated experience requirements. The Department of Employment and Labour advertises a State Accountant post in financial reporting, requiring an NQF level 6 qualification in accounting or finance with one year of experience, and a Senior State Accountant post requiring the same qualification level with two years of financial reporting experience. The Department of Correctional Services advertises a Human Resources Practitioner post in placement administration, requiring a human resources diploma or degree with one to two years of experience.</p>
<p>At the senior end, the Department of Correctional Services advertises an Assistant Director post for injury on duty, requiring a relevant qualification with three to five years of experience, and the Department of Employment and Labour advertises a Deputy Director post in fleet management at its Gauteng provincial office, requiring an NQF level 6 qualification with five years of specified experience. The Deputy Director post is a re-advertisement, so applicants who applied previously must submit a new application.</p>
<h2>DPSA vacancies: salaries at a glance</h2>
<p>The Deputy Director figure is an all-inclusive package, not basic pay alone.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Position</th><th>Department</th><th>Annual salary</th></tr>
<tr><td>Registry Clerk</td><td>Communications and Digital Technologies</td><td>R237,453</td></tr>
<tr><td>HR Practitioner</td><td>Correctional Services</td><td>R338,106</td></tr>
<tr><td>State Accountant</td><td>Employment and Labour</td><td>R338,106</td></tr>
<tr><td>Senior State Accountant</td><td>Employment and Labour</td><td>R413,001</td></tr>
<tr><td>Assistant Director: Injury on Duty</td><td>Correctional Services</td><td>R487,197</td></tr>
<tr><td>Deputy Director: Fleet Management</td><td>Employment and Labour</td><td>R932,292 (all-inclusive package)</td></tr>
</table>
<h2>Closing dates differ by department</h2>
<p>There is no single closing date for Circular 39, and this is where applicants most often go wrong. The Department of Electricity and Energy closes on 13 November 2026 at 16:00, and the Department of Employment and Labour closes on 13 November 2026, with 16:00 for walk-in submissions and 23:59 for online submissions. The Department of Communications and Digital Technologies closes on 16 November 2026, and the Department of Correctional Services closes on 16 November 2026 at 15:45. Other departments set their own deadlines, which appear in each advertisement.</p>
<p>The circular also carries amendments to earlier advertisements. The Independent Police Investigative Directorate has extended the closing date for two Director posts originally advertised in Circular 38, Director for Security and Facilities Management (reference Q9/2026/54) and Director for Risk and Integrity Management (reference Q9/2026/55), to 16 November 2026. Separately, the Department of Employment and Labour has withdrawn the Personnel Officer post at its East London provincial office (reference HR4/4/1/13), previously advertised in Circular 37.</p>
<p>Provincial sections cover the Eastern Cape (pages 157 to 191), the Free State (pages 192 to 209), Gauteng (pages 210 to 225), KwaZulu-Natal (pages 226 to 265), Mpumalanga (pages 266 to 267), the Northern Cape (pages 268 to 269) and the Western Cape (pages 270 to 309).</p>
<h2>What to do next</h2>
<p>The practical steps are straightforward, but they must be followed per post. First, download Circular 39 from the Department of Public Service and Administration vacancy circular pages, or open the departmental section for the department you want. Second, identify a post that matches your qualifications and experience, and note its title, reference number and closing date. Third, read the department's application instructions in full, including whether the new Z83 form is required and the submission channel. Fourth, prepare a comprehensive curriculum vitae showing your training, qualifications, competencies, knowledge and experience, and complete the Z83 form in full where required. Fifth, submit the application to the advertising department itself, before the stated closing date and time.</p>
<p>The same preparation rule applies elsewhere on this site. The <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/opm-federal-hiring-fair-29-october-20261028">federal hiring fair held in the United States on 29 October 2026</a> rewarded candidates who arrived with a finished resume and specific questions. For a broader picture of working abroad, including where sponsorship is realistic and where it is not, see our guide to <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/software-engineer-jobs-in-usa-for-foreigners-h-1b-pay-and-hiring-guide-20261029">software engineer jobs in the USA for foreign workers</a>.</p>
<h2>A note for readers in Nepal and abroad</h2>
<p>For readers in Nepal, the honest position is that DPSA vacancies are South African public service posts, advertised for applicants within South Africa's public service system, and they are not a migration or visa route. Anyone who promises to secure a South African government appointment for a fee, or asks for payment before an application, should be treated as a scam. Genuine applications go through the official departmental channels, cost nothing beyond the applicant's own preparation, and never require payment to an individual.</p>
<p>Start with one department and one post, rather than trying to read all 309 pages. For Matric holders, the Registry Clerk post is the clearest entry point, and the earliest highlighted deadlines fall on 13 November 2026.</p><p><small>Sources: Department of Public Service and Administration, Public Service Vacancy Circular 39 of 2026, published 30 October 2026, via the <a href="https://www.dpsa.gov.za/newsroom/psvc/">DPSA public service vacancy circular pages</a>; circular summary and highlighted posts as reported by <a href="https://gpgprofessional-jobcentre.co.za/dpsa-vacancy-circular-39-of-2026/">GPG Professional Job Centre</a> and the <a href="https://gpgprofessional-jobcentre.co.za/dcdt-registry-clerk-vacancy-2026-matric-no-experience/">Registry Clerk vacancy notice</a>, with departmental PDFs linked from dpsa.gov.za.</small></p>$$,
